## ABSTRACT

Humanity has consistently framed the challenge of becoming a multiplanetary species in technological terms: propulsion, life support, materials science, energy systems, and robotics. This framing, while technically necessary, is theoretically insufficient. The present thesis argues that the dominant bottleneck to a durable, self-sustaining multiplanetary civilization is not propulsive or energetic but fundamentally economic: the insufficient expansion of productive participation across the global human population.

This work introduces the Prosperity Engine, a formal self-reinforcing mechanism through which education, technological augmentation, entrepreneurship, and inclusive productivity transforms latent human potential into compounding civilizational capital. The Prosperity Engine operates through four coupled feedback loops: (i) human capital accumulation drives innovation intensity; (ii) innovation intensity expands market productivity; (iii) expanded productivity generates fiscal and private surplus; (iv) surplus finances both further human capital investment and large-scale scientific infrastructure — including space exploration. The critical feature of this mechanism is its nonlinearity: economic exclusion does not merely reduce output proportionally — it severs entire branches of the innovation tree, eliminating discoveries that compound through future knowledge.

The thesis grounds this argument in endogenous growth theory (Romer 1990; Lucas 1988), the empirical literature on innovation and inequality (Bell, Chetty, Jaravel, Petkova, and Van Reenen 2019), the political economy of technological leapfrogging, and the emerging economics of the space industry. It introduces the concept of civilizational innovation efficiency (CIE) — a measure of the ratio of realized to potential innovation output across a population — and demonstrates that current CIE is dramatically below optimum due to structural economic exclusion.

A second major contribution is the articulation of an AI Catalytic Acceleration Hypothesis (ACAH): that artificial intelligence, if deployed under conditions of active distributive policy, represents the first historical instrument capable of compressing the time required to move large populations from subsistence-level participation to productive economic contribution — collapsing what previously took generations into years. However, without deliberate policy architecture, AI risks reproducing and amplifying existing exclusions, producing what this thesis designates as a bifurcated civilizational trajectory: a narrow high-capability layer advancing toward space while billions remain economically inert.

The thesis concludes that the road to Mars is, at its deepest level, a road to global prosperity. A multiplanetary civilization is not built by a small number of engineers and billionaires. It is financed, staffed, and sustained by the productive participation of billions. Poverty is not merely a humanitarian failure. It is a civilizational inefficiency of the highest order.

