## CHAPTER 3: THE GLOBAL MIDDLE CLASS — STRUCTURE, TRAJECTORY, AND THEORETICAL STATUS

### 3.1 Defining the Middle Class for Civilizational Purposes

The concept of the "middle class" is notoriously difficult to define with precision. For the purposes of this thesis, we adopt a functionalist definition: the global middle class consists of individuals and households with sufficient income and security to engage in productive economic participation beyond bare subsistence — that is, who are able to invest in education, start or grow businesses, accumulate savings, and contribute to the formal economy as both producers and consumers.

This is distinct from purely income-based thresholds, though those inform it. The World Bank uses multiple poverty lines — now anchored at $3.00 per day for extreme poverty (2021 PPP), $4.20 for lower-middle-income thresholds, and $8.30 for upper-middle-income thresholds. For civilizational purposes, the relevant threshold is higher than any of these: it is the point at which individuals move from survival mode into a condition where they can meaningfully participate in the knowledge economy.

### 3.2 The Current State of Global Prosperity

The global distribution of prosperity presents a picture of dramatic progress accompanied by persistent and troubling concentrations of exclusion. On one hand, the historical trajectory is genuinely remarkable: the share of humanity living in extreme poverty has fallen from over 35 percent in 1990 to approximately 10 percent today.

On the other hand, in absolute terms, the numbers remain staggering. As of 2024, approximately 847 million people are estimated to live in extreme poverty, with the Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan, and Pakistan being the only region where the extreme poverty rate has increased in recent years. [World Bank](https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/opendata/march-2026-global-poverty-update-from-the-world-bank--new-data-a) More broadly, billions more live above the extreme poverty line but below any reasonable threshold of productive economic participation.

The trajectory on the middle class side is more encouraging. The middle class population in emerging markets is set to double over the next decade, expanding from 354 million households in 2024 to over 670 million households by 2035. [Oxford Economics](https://www.oxfordeconomics.com/resource/the-future-of-the-middle-class-in-emerging-markets-2026-update/) This expansion is geographically concentrated but rapidly broadening. More than half of the world's population is projected to be in the middle class by 2030. [Visual Capitalist](https://elements.visualcapitalist.com/the-worlds-growing-middle-class-2020-2030/)

The World Development Report 2024 frames the challenge facing middle-income countries in terms of the "middle-income trap" — the tendency of countries to get stuck at intermediate income levels, unable to make the transitions from investment-led to innovation-led growth. Middle-income countries — home today to 6 billion people — are in a race against time. Successful middle-income countries will have to engineer two successive transitions: first from investment-driven strategies to also adopting modern technologies, and second to accelerating the shift to innovation by pushing global technological frontiers. [World Bank](https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/wdr2024)

### 3.3 The Middle Class as Theoretical Economic Agent

From the perspective of civilizational economics, the middle class is not simply a demographic category but a mode of economic agency. Its theoretical significance operates through several channels.

First, as a demand generator. A middle class with discretionary income creates large, stable markets for goods and services. Market size is a fundamental determinant of innovation incentives in both Romer-type and Schmookler-type innovation models: the larger the market into which a successful innovation can be sold, the higher the expected returns to R&D investment, and therefore the more R&D occurs. A world with a larger middle class is a world with stronger innovation incentives.

Second, as a fiscal base. Governments derive revenue primarily from middle-class and upper-class incomes and consumption. A larger middle class means more fiscal capacity, which means more public investment in the human capital formation (education, health), scientific infrastructure (universities, research grants, space agencies), and institutional quality that support long-run growth.

Third, as an entrepreneurial reservoir. Small and medium enterprises, which generate the majority of employment and a substantial fraction of innovation in most economies, are predominantly middle-class enterprises. Entrepreneurs drawn from the middle class have enough economic security to take risks but are not so wealthy as to be insulated from market signals. This range of economic position is, empirically, the sweet spot for entrepreneurial activity.

Fourth, as a human capital accumulator. Middle-class households invest disproportionately in education, both for themselves and their children. Education is the primary vector through which individuals transition from purely productive labor into innovative labor — from executing existing processes to creating new ones.

### 3.4 The Middle-Income Trap as a Civilizational Bottleneck

If the argument of this thesis is correct — that a large productive middle class is a prerequisite for civilizational-scale projects — then the middle-income trap is not merely a development economics problem. It is a civilizational bottleneck. It is the force that keeps billions of people below the threshold of productive contribution to the innovation economy, not through lack of capability but through structural barriers to opportunity.

The World Development Report 2024 identifies the institutional and policy conditions that allow countries to escape the trap: openness to new technology, competitive markets that reward entrants, political systems that allow contestability of power, and energy systems that enable productivity growth. Transitions from investment-led growth through technology diffusion toward discovery of new ideas are not automatic. They involve step changes where economies need to shed institutions and policies that will not help in the stage ahead. [Worldbank](https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/ba24094c4f9f37714f07345b1505c930-0050062023/original/WDR2024-Concept-Note.pdf)

The implication for the Prosperity Engine framework is that removing the middle-income trap is not only a development goal — it is a precondition for assembling the civilizational coalition that a multiplanetary project requires.

