## CHAPTER 6: THE SPACE ECONOMY AND ITS ECONOMIC PREREQUISITES

### 6.1 The Current State of the Space Economy

The space economy of the mid-2020s is a genuine and rapidly growing sector, but it is still, by the standards of any serious multiplanetary civilization, embryonic. The global space economy was valued at $626 billion in 2025, roughly double its size a decade ago. The commercial sector accounts for approximately 78% of the total, with government budgets contributing the remaining 22%. The industry is expected to cross the $1 trillion mark between 2032 and 2034. [Orbitalradar](https://orbitalradar.com/space-economy)

Commercial activities now account for approximately 78% of the global space economy, up from approximately 60% a decade ago. [SpaceNexus](https://spacenexus.us/space-stats) The launch services market reached approximately $14 billion in 2025, with SpaceX commanding an overwhelming majority market share by launch count. Global government space spending exceeded $95 billion, supported by a workforce exceeding 360,000 in the United States alone. [SpaceNexus](https://spacenexus.us/space-stats)

These numbers are impressive as measures of the current sector. They are, however, far too small to sustain a genuine multiplanetary civilization. A colony of even a few tens of thousands on Mars — a rounding error by the standards of any terrestrial city — would require sustained logistical support at a scale that dwarfs any current space infrastructure.

### 6.2 The Scale Problem

Elon Musk has argued that to make life multiplanetary, rocket and spacecraft technology needs to improve by 1,000 times. He estimates that building a self-sustaining city on Mars would require at least a million tons of equipment, with the total price tag potentially reaching $1,000 trillion at current costs, though he believes this could be reduced to approximately $1 trillion with the needed technological improvements. [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/elon-musks-mars-mission-math-183016006.html)

Even under the most optimistic technological scenario — a 1,000x improvement in cost efficiency — the numbers are extraordinary. A trillion-dollar Mars colonization effort, spread over 40 years, implies roughly $25 billion per year. For context, NASA's current annual budget is approximately $25 billion, and the entire U.S. defense space budget is approximately $30 billion. A sustained Mars colony would consume all of current civil space spending, indefinitely.

More importantly, these numbers apply to an initial settlement phase. A genuine, self-sustaining, economically viable Martian civilization would need to be bootstrapped through a phase of massive Earth-based subsidy, after which it would need to develop its own economic base. While a Mars base of even a few hundred people can potentially be supported out of pocket by governmental expenditures, a Martian society of hundreds of thousands clearly cannot be. To be viable, a real Martian civilization must either be completely autarchic or be able to produce some kind of export that allows it to pay for the imports it requires. [Aleph](https://www.aleph.se/Trans/Tech/Space/mars.html)

### 6.3 The Economic Foundation of Space Ambition

The core argument connecting the Prosperity Engine to the space economy is this: the Earth-based surplus necessary to finance, staff, and sustain a multiplanetary program scales with the breadth and productivity of Earth's economic base. A wealthier, more broadly participatory global economy can:

(i) Generate more fiscal surplus available for long-horizon public investment;
(ii) Produce a larger pool of STEM-trained individuals from which to draw engineers, scientists, and operational staff;
(iii) Create a larger commercial market for space-adjacent technologies (satellite communications, Earth observation, precision navigation) that drives down costs through scale economies;
(iv) Foster the entrepreneurial ecosystem that produces the SpaceX-type companies that dramatically reduce launch costs through commercial innovation;
(v) Generate the political consensus across diverse societies necessary to sustain multi-decade, multi-trillion dollar commitments.

None of these conditions can be met by a civilization that leaves half or more of its population outside the productive economic system. Robert Zubrin argues that the long-term goal of establishing a colony on Mars will require private-sector innovation and investment. He further observes that the challenging living conditions on Mars and the constant need to find novel solutions to problems would lead to an innovative boost that would ultimately also benefit Earth's economy. [Townhall](https://townhall.com/columnists/rainerzitelmann/2026/05/24/mars-colonization-and-the-economic-future-of-spacex-n2676588) But the private-sector innovation ecosystem that Zubrin envisions is precisely what the Prosperity Engine framework predicts will emerge from a broad and participatory global economy.

### 6.4 The Space Economy as a Prosperity Dividend

The relationship between terrestrial prosperity and space capability is not one-directional. Space activities generate what can be called a Prosperity Dividend — a stream of economic benefits that flow back to the terrestrial economy in the form of new technologies, new industries, satellite-based services, and the economic activity surrounding the space sector itself.

Technological advancements in reusable launch vehicles, smaller satellites, and digital payloads are driving down costs and enhancing scalability of missions. Beyond satellite imagery, rising demand for navigation, broadband connectivity, climate monitoring, and defense-related applications also support the space economy. [Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/space-economy-market-size-reach-130000220.html)

Of particular relevance to the Prosperity Engine is satellite-based broadband. The deployment of mega-constellations like Starlink creates a potential mechanism for leapfrogging terrestrial connectivity gaps in low-income countries, providing internet access — and through it, access to education, financial services, markets, and economic participation — to populations currently excluded by the absence of ground-based infrastructure. This creates a positive feedback loop: space investment → satellite broadband → economic inclusion → more productive global economy → more space investment.

