## CHAPTER 8: TECHNOLOGICAL LEAPFROGGING AND THE NEW GEOGRAPHY OF INNOVATION

### 8.1 The Leapfrogging Phenomenon

Technological leapfrogging refers to the ability of late-developing countries or regions to bypass intermediate stages of technological development by adopting more advanced technologies that did not exist when earlier adopters established their infrastructure. The phenomenon is not new — Japan famously leapfrogged Western industrialization by importing and improving manufacturing technologies in the post-war period — but it has taken on new dimensions in the digital era.

Africa's mobile revolution is a leading example of leapfrogging: fixed-line access has remained minimal, while mobile subscriptions have surged dramatically. Internet penetration, though still low at 35% in 2024, has grown rapidly from just 7.5% in 2009. Mobile technology has transformed access to financial services, enabling millions of unbanked people to use payments, savings, credit and insurance services without traditional banks. Sub-Saharan Africa leads globally, with over 1 billion mobile money accounts and 280 million active users. Annual transaction values exceeded $1 trillion by 2025. [ISS African Futures](https://futures.issafrica.org/thematic/09-leapfrog/)

This example is instructive for the Prosperity Engine framework in several ways. First, it demonstrates that excluded populations are not passive — they adopt and innovate around new technologies at remarkable speed when those technologies are accessible and relevant to their needs. Second, it demonstrates that leapfrogging is not merely catching up — it can produce genuinely novel economic arrangements that are not merely inferior copies of developed-world systems but new institutional configurations adapted to local conditions.

### 8.2 Fintech and Financial Inclusion as a Prosperity Engine Case Study

The transformation of financial inclusion through mobile money in Sub-Saharan Africa provides perhaps the most compelling real-world illustration of the Prosperity Engine dynamics in action.

In 2024, Africa's digital payment networks surpassed 1.1 billion mobile users, facilitating over $1.1 trillion in transactions, a milestone that integrated millions into the global economy. Latin America's fintech sector saw funding surge 73% in 2024, cementing its role as a financial innovation hub, while Southeast Asia's expanding AI talent pool accelerates cross-border collaboration. [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org/stories/2025/07/africa-leapfrog-moment-harnessing-technology-green-growth-and-regional-integration-for-global-value-chains/)

Financial inclusion unlocks the Entrepreneurship Bottleneck described in Chapter 5. When individuals have access to savings, credit, payments, and insurance — previously available only through formal banking systems that excluded the poor — they gain the capital threshold prerequisites for enterprise formation. A McKinsey report predicts that fintech could add up to $150 billion to Africa's GDP by 2027, driven by deeper financial inclusion and innovation. [Worldecomag](https://worldecomag.com/africa-mobile-money-revolution/)

This case study illustrates the Prosperity Engine loop operating at a regional scale: technology-enabled financial access → more entrepreneurial activity → more productive employment → more income → more savings and investment → more demand for further technological services → further innovation in those services.

Africa has already been on the leading edge of using technology to leapfrog traditional development paths, bypassing landlines and moving straight to mobile phones, and adopting e-banking and mobile money while traditional banking rates were still low. Home to the world's largest free-trade area, and with the highest rates of entrepreneurship, African tech creators are already generating some of the most exciting innovations in the world. [Institute Global](https://institute.global/insights/tech-and-digitalisation/supercharging-africas-startups-continents-path-tech-excellence)

### 8.3 The Leapfrogging Condition for AI

The leapfrogging literature identifies several conditions under which late adopters can successfully bypass intermediate development stages. These conditions map onto the ACAH analysis in Chapter 7. For AI to serve as a leapfrogging technology for low-income populations, it requires:

(i) Accessible hardware infrastructure: smartphones or equivalent devices capable of running or accessing AI services;
(ii) Affordable connectivity: internet access at costs compatible with low-income budgets;
(iii) Relevant AI services: AI applications designed for the problems and contexts of low-income users, not merely adapted from high-income contexts;
(iv) Local language support: AI systems that function in the languages and dialects of the relevant populations;
(v) Digital literacy: basic competencies to use AI tools productively.

Where these conditions are met, AI has the potential to deliver on the ACAH. Where they are not, the divergence risk dominates. This creates a clear policy agenda for the nations and institutions that want to see the Prosperity Engine accelerate: invest systematically in meeting the leapfrogging conditions for AI, treating them as the critical path infrastructure for civilizational progress.

### 8.4 The Innovation Geography Shift

One often-underappreciated implication of leapfrogging dynamics is the potential shift in the geography of innovation. The historical concentration of innovative activity in a small number of high-income countries and metropolitan centers — Silicon Valley, Boston, London, Tokyo, Seoul — is partly a function of where the thresholds for economic participation have historically been met. If AI and digital connectivity shift those thresholds, reducing the minimum requirements for effective innovation participation, the geography of innovation becomes more distributed.

The Global Innovation Index 2025 provides comprehensive analysis across 139 economies. While technology adoption expanded across all indicators in 2024, every single metric fell short of its long-term growth trend — a clear signal that adoption momentum is decelerating despite continued technological progress. [WIPO](https://www.wipo.int/web-publications/global-innovation-index-2025/en/gii-2025-at-a-glance.html)

The deceleration in innovation adoption growth — documented across EV expansion, 5G deployment, and industrial robotics — is consistent with a system approaching the limits of its current innovation base. The historical innovation geography has, in effect, mined the available talent within its geographic and economic reach. The next frontier of innovation growth requires expanding that geographic and economic reach — precisely what the Prosperity Engine framework prescribes.

